   840	def _headers(iss, rec, a, heights):
     1	"""Request store and result store behind interfaces, with in-memory implementations (AWS ones in aws.py).
     2	
     3	Request store: request records (state, content digest, timestamps, pipeline progress; never content), content-free
     4	key tombstones kept until issuance closes, the signer-request index (signer request id -> request and job), and the
     5	append-only signature log (E-02). Records carry a version; save() is a compare-and-set on it.
     6	
     7	Result store: blobs in four namespaces: "content" (submitted bytes, deleted when the request ends), "work" (frames,
     8	signatures, proofs; deleted when the request ends), "frames" (frames too large for a queue message, read by the
     9	signers by digest-bound reference; deleted when the request ends) and "result" (completed receipts, deleted 30 days
    10	after the end).
    11	
    12	Cleanup obligations (IS-07) are kept apart from the request record, so a failed deletion is retried by the poller
    13	whatever state the request is in, and the 30-day deletion of results does not depend on S3 lifecycle timing.
    14	
    15	Lifecycle guard (design steps 1 and 5): every lifecycle write is a compare-and-set on the record version AND on the
    16	expected (request state, current attempt id, current attempt state), evaluated atomically by the store, so a write
    17	meant for an ended attempt, or decided on a version it did not read, fails (the model's GuardOK).
    18	
    19	Storage lifetime (design revision 5.3.1): no age rule applies to a live request's objects. When the cleanup
    20	obligation finds deletion due, it first marks the objects `cleanup-due` (mark_due), then deletes them; an S3
    21	lifecycle rule expires only marked objects, as a backstop for deletions that keep failing."""
    22	import copy
    23	
    24	CLEANUP_TAG = ("cleanup-due", "true")
