    76	- **IS-04 (tombstone):** the request record and its key tombstone are written in one DynamoDB transaction. A
    77	  repeat submission repairs a missing tombstone.
    78	- **IS-06 (retrieval; owner decision, supersedes the earlier Range approach):** a receipt (about 5 MB, since every
    79	  bundle carries the frozen spec and vectors) is retrieved with ONE plain GET, as docs/V1-SERVICE-RESOLUTIONS.md
    80	  S2/S5 describe. `GET /v1/requests/{key}/receipt` checks that the caller's credential owns the request and that it
    81	  is COMPLETE and not EXPIRED; only then it mints a fresh pre-signed S3 GET URL valid for 300 seconds and answers
    82	  303 to it, with the receipt's SHA-256 in `Tessera-Receipt-SHA256`. No URL is minted for another credential (404),
    83	  an expired request (410) or a non-complete one (409); the lifetime is never above 300 s; minted URLs are never
    84	  stored or reused. The receipt object is deleted 30 days after the end, so later URLs fail. The Range requirement
    85	  and 416 `RANGE_REQUIRED` are removed.
    86	- **IS-07 (deletion):** S3 lifecycle is no longer the deletion mechanism. Ending a request first records a
    87	  cleanup obligation (`CLEAN#<rid>`, kept apart from the record). Each poll retries the obligation until a listing
    88	  shows nothing left: content, work files and frames at the end; the receipt 30 days after the end. Lifecycle
    89	  rules stay as a 31-day backstop. *(Superseded 2026-10-30, revision 5.3.1: no age rule; the backstop expires
    90	  only objects the obligation has tagged cleanup-due.)*
    91	- **IS-09 (large frames):** a signer request whose inline message would exceed 256 KiB carries `frame_ref` (an
    92	  immutable object `frames/<rid>/<sha256>`) and `frame_sha256` instead of `frame_b64`. The signer refuses unless
    93	  the object's SHA-256 matches. Signer roles get read access to `frames/*` only. **Open:** the deployed signers
    94	  must accept `frame_ref`.
    95	- **IS-01, IS-08 (signature bookkeeping):** a verified response is logged as "used" before its signature becomes
    96	  eligible. Each signature is stored under its own name (`sig-<job>-<sha256>`), and its job is merged into the
    97	  record with compare-and-set retries. Sealing requires the log record. Before any re-send, a stored signature
