| Acknowledgement and retry | KMS mapping deletes successful handled messages and reports partial failures (`lambda_app.py:73-90`; `infra/main.tf:151-155`). GCP sends answer before request deletion (`signer.py:192-193`); failed send/delete permits redelivery. Issuer acknowledges only after `_take` finishes (`pipeline.py:424-432`). **Agree with at-least-once delivery**, not exactly-once signing. | K-slot/K-full mapping fake; G-slot fake SQS; GCP signer-only failed-delete and retry tests. |

### Infrastructure permissions and configuration

The poller writes frame objects, sends each slot'"'s requests, and receives/deletes responses ("'`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:237-262`). The API does not need signer-queue or signing grants. Result-bucket encryption is AES256 (`aws.py:231`), so the reference-read design does not require a separate SSE-KMS decrypt grant.

KMS Lambda role: `service/signer-kms/infra/main.tf:100-115` grants request Receive/Delete/GetQueueAttributes, response Send, and GetObject on `${frames_bucket}/frames/*`. `FRAMES_BUCKET` is supplied in `:16-22`, consumed by `lambda_app.py:68-69`. Its event source reads the issuer'"'s kms request queue ("'`:151-155`). It does not need the issuer'"'s remote signer-role assumption; the KMS README explicitly excludes "'`kms` from `signer_principals` (`:53-54`).

GCP: there is no `service/signer-gcp/infra/`. `deploy.sh:108-131` constructs the Google web-identity federated role and its permission to assume the issuer'"'s gpg signer role; "'`:139-142` configures both roles, queues and FRAME_BUCKET. The issuer'"'s "'`signer_principals` trust and policy (`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:289-319`) supply per-slot request/response access and GetObject on `${results_bucket}/frames/*`. Runtime assumes the configured roles before use (`service/signer-gcp/tessera_signer/main.py:87-95`). GCP'"'s HSM IAM is separate from AWS frame access.

**Repository grants agree with the read paths.** None grants signers object writes or depends on an attempt-prefixed name. They are source inspections backed by static infra/adapter tests, not an applied-plan or live-principal proof. Bucket, role, queue, pinned key/creation time and SIGN_UNTIL still have to be wired consistently at deployment; no test establishes that those deployed values match the issuer window.

## C. Harness changes

The unpacking edits discard only the new age key; they retain status, signature verification, duplicate-signature identity and queue-empty assertions ("'`service/signer-kms/tests/test_roundtrip.py:64-70`; `service/signer-gcp/tests/test_issuer_protocol.py:26-27,76-84`). They do not change signer response handling or signer retry thresholds.

GCP `QueueView.receive` now requests the real count from FakeSqs and places it in the issuer'"'s Message ("'`tests/fakes.py:93-99`). FakeSqs increments and returns the count only when requested (`:50-62`). This strengthens fidelity; it does not hard-code every delivery as first. The scratch check deliberately omitted acknowledgement and advanced past visibility: receives changed **1 → 2**. None of the old receive-count cases was removed. Remaining limitation: sent=None and seq=0 mean this test wrapper cannot test cross-slot oldest-first order; that was not previously covered by these tests either.

## Appended owner prechecks

| Check | Result | Evidence and consequence |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Capture row | **PASS** for the reviewed interface claims and this report. | `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md:34-37,50-53,72-74` governs the boundary: fresh attacker-key receipts can pass after DNS+git capture; no capture detection/public visibility is promised. Pinning each configured signer'"'s key and checking its frame does not add a service-key listing or prevent attacker-controlled alternate authority/key material from verifying. |
| 2. Trigger clause | **PASS** for the fix's operative conclusions. | Cleanup requires request termination ("'`pipeline.py:317-329`), not an ended attempt. Refusal-to-discard requires indexed current waiting job (`:479-500`); signing closure is evaluated after recovery (`:681-687`). N-exhaustion is tested only after ending N (`:296-301`). No sampled-unavailability trigger is inferred from age, failure count or the frame rename. Amendment 11 remains explicitly unsigned (`:3-10`); its proposed consequences are not invoked as adopted authority here. |
| 3. Requester | **PASS**, bounded to source configuration. | The issuer can cause both signatures by writing policy-conforming requests (`issuer/infra/main.tf:254-257`; `pipeline.py:596`). Anybody else with queue-send or direct invocation/role/code/key-management capabilities may also cause work or change policy. The KMS policy restricts what passes, not whose content it is (`signer-kms/README.md:61-65`). Deployment requester enumeration is still required (`issuer/DEPLOY.md:66-71`), not established by key non-exportability or these tests. Issuing-account compromise remains unprotected (`V1-CONTRACT.md:73`). |
| 4. Binding text | **FAIL — pre-existing, not caused by FIX.diff.** | ISI-02: dedicated signer-role placement differs from registered `HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md:173-174`; unsigned A11 `:240-250` cannot amend it yet. Gated indefinite-open behavior (`pipeline.py:46-49`; lifecycle decision `:189-196`) does not discharge A2.3 `:288-289`. The frame-reference format and INLINE_MAX are engineering contracts being compared for interoperability, not promoted into registered security requirements. No schedule ruling-budget or 24-hour FAILED rule is used as authority. |

## Validation and conclusion

Executed `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q` separately in all four package directories. Final counts are recorded below after completion. The scratch probe uses both production signer classes with the real issuer local pipeline and its TEST window keys, while replacing provider clients and queues. Both sizes reached COMPLETE through actual pre-ship checking, and both GCP attestation and manifest were signed. This is local execution evidence; it does not establish deployed code identity, live IAM, model refinement, general liveness, or registration adoption.

The fix is appropriate to keep. No regression was found in attempt isolation, frame binding, cleanup, signature storage, or harness receive counts. Retain the GCP pipeline integration coverage to prevent the same defect class from recurring, and keep the existing registration decisions separate from this functional confirmation.
